"use strict";(self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara=self.webpackChunknotes_by_sara||[]).push([["7542"],{6042(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"FIN-034","slug":"trade-off","term":"Trade-Off","also_known_as":[],"difficulty":"Beginner","definition":"A trade-off is a situation where gaining more of one thing means accepting less of another. It is the balancing act involved in almost every financial decision.","explanation":["Because money, time and resources are limited, choices involve giving something up. A trade-off describes that exchange. Opportunity cost measures what is given up; the trade-off is the choice itself.","Finance is full of trade-offs. Saving more today means spending less now but having more later. A safe savings account offers certainty but low returns, while shares offer higher expected returns but more ups and downs. A longer loan term lowers monthly payments but increases the total interest paid.","For example, a 10,000 loan at 6% repaid over three years costs about 304 a month and about 952 in total interest. Spreading the same loan over five years lowers the monthly payment to about 193, but total interest rises to roughly 1,600. Neither option is wrong; each suits different priorities.","Recognising trade-offs helps people make deliberate decisions rather than assuming there is a perfect option with no downside. In finance, an offer that claims high return with no risk, or high reward with no cost, deserves careful checking."],"note":"","total":1500,"prev":{"slug":"trade-finance","term":"Trade Finance"},"next":{"slug":"trade-off-theory-of-capital-structure","term":"Trade-Off Theory of Capital Structure"}}')}}]);